-- For an HTTP/1.1 server on boc's behaviours: a listener's connections
-- served at once, as many as come, each step of each a behaviour, and
-- every wait (an accept, a read, a write) an act awaited (`BOC.await`),
-- so no thread and no cown is held while a client is slow.  Each act
-- waits in the reactor of a thread of the program's pool of boc's, and
-- the connections are served in parallel on as many threads as the
-- machine has cores.  It is the C backend's alone, as std.net is.
-- Import it by a name, `import std.http.bocserver as BocServer`.
--
-- > [!CAUTION]
-- > Unstable: concurrency in heff is not settled. What this module offers
-- > may change, or go, in any release.
--
-- ```haskell
-- import boc as BOC
-- import co
-- import custodian as Custodian
-- import exception
-- import std.http as HTTP
-- import std.http.bocserver as BocServer
-- import std.net as Net
-- import std.signal as Signal
-- import std.time as Time
-- import resource
--
-- -- | each request answered at once
-- hello : {e : HFunctor}
--       -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
--       -> HTTP.ServerRequest
--       -> BocServer.Responder e Empty
--       -> Co (exception Empty <+> e) Unit
-- hello r respond = respond (Whole (HTTP.plain 200 ("hello, " ^ r.target ^ "\n")))
--
-- -- | the listener served until SIGTERM or Ctrl-C: then closed, the
-- -- | requests in hand answered, and every connection closed
-- served : {e : HFunctor}
--        -> {s : Type}
--        -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
--        -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
--        -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
--        -> {{sg : Signal.signals <: e}}
--        -> With s Net.TcpListener
--        -> Co e Unit
-- served l = do
--   BocServer.serve Empty l (hello (e := e))
--   with _ := BOC.await (Signal.wait [Terminate, Interrupt]) do
--     _ <- Net.closeListener l
--     pure ()
--
-- -- | served on port 8080, its behaviours run on a pool's threads, each
-- -- | waiting on the world through a reactor of its own; done once they
-- -- | are
-- helloServed : Co
--               (BOC.boc
--                <+> Custodian.custodian
--                <+> Net.net
--                <+> Time.time
--                <+> Signal.signals)
--               Unit
-- helloServed = do
--   r <-
--     Net.withListener
--         { host = "127.0.0.1", port = 8080 }
--         128
--         served
--   pure ()
-- ```
--
-- `serve err l handle` comes back at once, the server running in its
-- behaviours: the listener, held by its `with`, is let go of by the
-- last of the server's acts to hold it, once the server is done; the
-- program ends then, if nothing else is pending.  A connection is had by
-- std.net's `accepted`, in the listener's region, and closed by the
-- server: when its client ends it or fails, when a request asks
-- (HTTP/1.0, `Connection: close`), or when the server stops.
--
-- A connection is two cowns: its inbox, the side that reads, and its
-- outbox, the side that writes.  Where the connection is, is which
-- behaviour comes next on it.  A read awaited comes back as a behaviour
-- on the inbox, which takes the bytes as far as they go and awaits the
-- next read, until the request is all read; then it hands the request
-- to the handler, with a responder.  The responder puts the answer in
-- the outbox, by a behaviour there, from whichever world it is called
-- in: there and then, or later, from another behaviour -- after acts of
-- the handler's own awaited, a request of std.http, a query -- the
-- connection meanwhile read no further.  An act the handler awaits is
-- of its world, its `with`'s (boc's `await`), an `attempt`'s around it
-- too.  Its first answer is taken; one after it is not.  A handler that
-- never responds leaves its connection open until the program lets go of
-- it.
--
-- The outbox holds what is to be written, in order, and its writer: a
-- behaviour of the server's world waiting there, `once`, for something
-- to write.  It writes what the socket takes at once, there; the rest
-- by a write it awaits, its behaviour on the outbox once it is done.
-- One write is awaited at a time -- the behaviours after writes awaited
-- come in the order the writes are done, not the order they were begun
-- -- and what is put in the outbox meanwhile waits for it, but for more
-- than the most, which closes the connection at once.  An answer written, the writer awaits the
-- next request's read: what comes after on the connection is the
-- server's, not the handler's, so the handler's `attempt`, around its
-- behaviours, is not around the next request's.  The two sides share
-- no behaviour but those that close the connection or stop it, which
-- are on both at once.
--
-- An answer is one of three.  A response, whole (`Whole`).  A stream
-- (`Streamed`): a status and headers, written at once -- in chunks to
-- HTTP/1.1, `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`; to HTTP/1.0, no length, the
-- connection closed at the end; to a `HEAD`, or of a status with no body,
-- the head alone -- then a body sent a piece at a time by a sender.  Or
-- the connection taken for WebSocket (`Upgraded`): the request checked
-- as std.http.websocket's `handshake` checks it, answered by its refusal
-- where it is refused, else by `101 Switching Protocols`, then messages
-- both ways.  A stream's or a socket's handler is given its sender in
-- `opened`, by the writer, once the head is written: a value -- no
-- region in its type -- kept anywhere, in a cown of the handler's beside
-- others, and sent by from any behaviour (`send`), each piece a
-- behaviour on the outbox, put there in the order sent and written
-- after the ones before it, never waited for by the sender.  `finish`
-- ends a stream: its last chunk written, the connection is read on for
-- the next request.  A socket's sender sends messages, each written as a
-- frame; its `finish` closes it, `1000`.
--
-- ```haskell
-- import boc as BOC
-- import bytes as Bytes
-- import co
-- import exception
-- import std.http as HTTP
-- import std.http.bocserver as BocServer
-- import std.http.sse as SSE
-- import std.http.websocket as WebSocket
-- import std.ordmap as OrdMap
--
-- -- | the senders of the event streams open, by their numbers
-- type Hub = BOC.Cown (OrdMap.OrdMap Int (BocServer.Sender Bytes))
--
-- -- | an event sent on each of them
-- told : {f : HFunctor}
--      -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}}
--      -> Bytes
--      -> List (Pair Int (BocServer.Sender Bytes))
--      -> Co f Unit
-- told b [] = pure ()
-- told b (MkPair n out :: rest) = do
--   BocServer.send out b
--   told b rest
--
-- -- | a stream kept in the hub
-- joined : {f : HFunctor} -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}} -> Hub -> BocServer.Sender Bytes -> Co f Unit
-- joined hub out = do
--   with h := hub do
--     h := OrdMap.insert h (BocServer.senderNumber out) out
--
-- -- | a stream gone, taken out of the hub
-- left : {f : HFunctor} -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}} -> Hub -> BocServer.Sender Bytes -> Co f Unit
-- left hub out = do
--   with h := hub do
--     h := OrdMap.delete h (BocServer.senderNumber out)
--
-- -- | `/events`, a stream of events, kept in the hub until it is gone;
-- -- | `/say`, its body an event to every stream there; `/echo`, a
-- -- | socket, each message sent back
-- chat : {e : HFunctor}
--      -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
--      -> Hub
--      -> HTTP.ServerRequest
--      -> BocServer.Responder e Empty
--      -> Co (exception Empty <+> e) Unit
-- chat hub r respond =
--   if r.target == "/events"
--   then respond
--       (Streamed
--          { status = 200, headers = SSE.headers }
--          { opened = joined hub, gone = left hub })
--   else if r.target == "/say"
--   then do
--     with h := hub do
--       told (SSE.event [Data (Bytes.asString r.body)]) (OrdMap.toList h)
--     respond (Whole (HTTP.plain 200 "said\n"))
--   else if r.target == "/echo"
--   then respond
--       (Upgraded
--          { opened = \out -> pure ()
--          , received = \out m -> BocServer.send out m
--          , gone = \out -> pure ()
--          })
--   else respond (Whole (HTTP.plain 404 "no such page\n"))
-- ```
--
-- Once the client is gone -- a write failed, its end read, its
-- socket closed -- the connection is closed, a sender's `send` does
-- nothing, and the handler's `gone` is called, once, by the behaviour
-- that closes it: so a hub of senders takes it out.  A stream's `gone`
-- is not called once it is finished; a socket's is, at its end, whyever
-- it ends.  What is sent and not yet written, on a connection, is
-- `pending` bytes at most -- a mebibyte unless another is given, by
-- name, `BocServer.serve (pending := 65536) err l handle`; 0 for no
-- limit -- past which the connection is closed, its client too slow, and
-- `gone` called: a sender is never made to wait.  A stream's connection
-- is read while it goes on, a piece at a time, on the inbox: where the
-- client closes it, it is gone at once, not at the next write.  What the
-- client sends then is the next request's, kept for after the stream;
-- with it kept, the connection is read no further until the stream is
-- finished, and the read waiting when it is finished is the next
-- request's.
--
-- A stream of server-sent events (its `Content-Type`
-- `text/event-stream`, std.http.sse's `headers`) is sent a comment, `:`,
-- after every `heartbeat` milliseconds of the client's silence where
-- nothing else was sent since the last -- 15 seconds unless another is
-- given, by name; 0 for none -- which keeps it from being taken for a
-- dead one by what is between, and tells the server soon after the
-- client has gone, as its write fails.  The wait is the stream's read: a
-- read, its time limit `heartbeat`.
--
-- A socket's frames are read an act at a time, on the inbox, each
-- read's bytes given to std.http.websocket's `feed`: each message of
-- text or of bytes the client sends is given to the handler's
-- `received` with the sender, in the order sent, by the behaviour that
-- read it.  A ping is answered by a pong; a close by a close, the same
-- code, then the connection closed once it is written; a frame that
-- breaks the protocol by a close of the code `feed` says, then the same.
-- A socket is not held to `idle`: where its client is silent for `ping`
-- milliseconds -- 30 seconds unless another is given, by name; 0 for
-- none -- it is sent a ping, and, silent for as long again, closed.  A
-- close sent by the handler (`finish`, or a `CloseMessage` sent) is
-- answered by the client's, and the connection closed then, or after
-- `ping` milliseconds.  `opened`, `received` and `gone` run in the
-- server's world, as the handler does: one that throws an `err`
-- closes its connection.
--
-- Requests are read as std.http reads them, by its `requestIn`,
-- a piece at a time, each piece an act: a body by `Content-Length`, or
-- sent in chunks, each piece read undone as it comes, a `100 Continue`
-- put in the outbox where the client waits for one, a request refused as
-- std.http refuses it (`400`, `413`, `431`, `501`, `505`), what
-- the client still sends then read, up to a mebibyte, and let go of
-- before its connection is closed.  Requests sent one after
-- another on a connection without waiting (pipelined) are answered in
-- turn.  A connection whose client sends nothing for `idle`
-- milliseconds -- 30 seconds unless another is given, by name,
-- `BocServer.serve (idle := 5000) l handle`; 0 for no limit -- is
-- closed, within a request or between two; a response not taken in that
-- time too, a piece of a stream or a frame of a socket too.  A client
-- that is not silent but sends a request's head a little at a time,
-- which `idle` alone would let hold its connection for ever, is held to
-- `headerWithin` milliseconds for the whole head -- 30 seconds unless
-- another is given, by name; 0 for no limit -- counted from the
-- connection's accept, or from the response before it (so on a
-- connection kept open, the wait for the next request counts too): past
-- that, the connection is closed, unanswered, as one silent too long
-- is.  The time is std.time's monotonic clock, so the server's world has
-- `time`.  A head's end is looked for in each piece only as it is read,
-- with the three bytes before it, and the head is parsed once, when all
-- of it is read.
--
-- The server stops where a connection cannot be accepted: the listener
-- closed (`Net.closeListener`, as above on a signal), or the program
-- out of descriptors (`EMFILE`).  Then each connection is stopped by a
-- behaviour on both its cowns: one waiting for a request is closed at
-- once; a stream is ended (its last chunk), a socket closed (`1001`),
-- each once what was sent on it is written, `gone` called; the others
-- are closed once the request in hand is answered.  Once the last is
-- closed, nothing of the server is pending, and the program ends, if
-- nothing else is.  The handler runs in a behaviour, of the server's
-- world, and may throw an `err` (`Empty` where it throws nothing): a
-- throw no `recover` of the handler's hears closes that connection at
-- once, unanswered, what was thrown let go of -- a handler that is to
-- say why catches it itself -- and the others are served on.  A handler
-- that fails otherwise (abandons) fails its behaviour, which boc's
-- `failed` hears: its request is not answered, and its connection, read
-- no further, is closed where the program lets go of it, at the run's
-- end.

module std.http.bocserver
  ( serve
  , Responder
  , Answer(..)
  , Streaming
  , Socket
  , Sender
  , send
  , finish
  , senderNumber
  )
import boc as BOC
import bytes as Bytes
import co
import exception
import std.duration as Duration
import std.http as HTTP
import std.http.sse as SSE
import std.http.websocket as WebSocket
import std.net as Net
import std.ordmap as OrdMap
import std.time as Time
import resource

-- Senders
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | What a handler sends its client by, on an answer that goes on: a
-- | stream's pieces (`Bytes`), or a socket's messages
-- | (`WebSocket.Message`).  A value, its connection's outbox within it
-- | -- no region in its type -- so kept anywhere, a cown of the
-- | handler's among others, and sent by from any behaviour.
data Sender a
  = MkSender Int ({f : HFunctor}
                  -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}}
                  -> Maybe a
                  -> Co f Unit)

-- | `x` sent: put in its connection's outbox, by a behaviour there, to
-- | be written after what was sent before it, by the server; the write
-- | not waited for.  A stream's piece is written as a chunk, a socket's
-- | message as a frame (a `CloseMessage` closes the socket).  Once the
-- | connection is gone, or the sender finished, nothing is done.
send : {f : HFunctor}
     -> {a : Type}
     -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}}
     -> Sender a
     -> a
     -> Co f Unit
send (MkSender n go) x = go (Just x)

-- | The sender done with, after what was sent before: a stream's last
-- | chunk written, its connection read on for the next request (closed
-- | after an HTTP/1.0 one's); a socket closed, `1000`.  Once the
-- | connection is gone, or the sender finished, nothing is done.
finish : {f : HFunctor}
       -> {a : Type}
       -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}}
       -> Sender a
       -> Co f Unit
finish (MkSender n go) = go Nothing

-- | The sender's number: its connection's, among the server's -- for a
-- | handler to keep its senders by, no two connections open at once
-- | numbered the same.
senderNumber : {a : Type} -> Sender a -> Int
senderNumber (MkSender n go) = n

-- Answers
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- | What a handler does with a stream, by its sender: `opened`, once
-- | its head is written, before any piece; `gone`, once its connection
-- | is gone before it is finished -- its client gone, or too slow, or the
-- | server stopped.  Each is run in a behaviour of the server's world: a
-- | throw closes the connection.
type Streaming (e : HFunctor) (err : Type) =
  { opened : Sender Bytes -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit
  , gone : Sender Bytes -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit
  }

-- | What a handler does with a socket, by its sender: `opened`, once
-- | the `101` is written; `received`, each message of text or of bytes
-- | the client sends, in order; `gone`, once its connection is closed,
-- | whyever.  Each is run in a behaviour of the server's world: a throw
-- | closes the connection.
type Socket (e : HFunctor) (err : Type) =
  { opened : Sender WebSocket.Message -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit
  , received :
        Sender WebSocket.Message
        -> WebSocket.Message
        -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit
  , gone : Sender WebSocket.Message -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit
  }

-- | What a handler answers its request with: a response, whole
-- | (`Whole`); a head (std.http's, a status and headers), then a
-- | body sent a piece at a time (`Streamed`); or the connection taken for WebSocket, where the
-- | request is a handshake (`Upgraded`).  A stream's headers give no
-- | length, `Transfer-Encoding` or `Connection` of their own -- the
-- | server writes those -- but `Connection: close`, which closes the
-- | connection after the stream.
data Answer (e : HFunctor) (err : Type)
  = Whole HTTP.Response
  | Streamed HTTP.Head (Streaming e err)
  | Upgraded (Socket e err)

-- | How a handler answers its request: in whichever world has boc's
-- | behaviours, its own or a later behaviour's.
type Responder (e : HFunctor) (err : Type) =
  {f : HFunctor} -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}} -> Answer e err -> Co f Unit

-- The connection's cowns
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (how a stream is written: in chunks or not, its connection closed
-- after it or not, and its heartbeat as written, none where empty)
type Way = { chunked : Bool, close : Bool, beat : Bytes }

-- (what the outbox has to do, in order: bytes written; once what is
-- before it is written, the next request read, from what was read after
-- the last (`ReadOn`); the connection closed (`Shut`); a stream begun, of
-- answer `g`, what was read after its request kept for after it
-- (`Opens`); a socket begun, of answer `g`, what was read after its
-- request its first frames (`Upgrades`); or a stream's end written, its
-- connection closed after it where it says (`Finished`))
data Out (e : HFunctor) (err : Type)
  = Writes Bytes
  | ReadOn Bytes
  | Shut
  | Opens Int Bytes Way (Streaming e err)
  | Upgrades Int Bytes (Socket e err)
  | Finished Bool

-- (a connection's outbox: what it has to do, the last first; how many
-- bytes are put there or being written; whether a write is awaited --
-- one at a time, for the behaviours after writes awaited come in the
-- order the writes are done; whether more than the most was
-- sent, for the connection to be closed at once; how many answers were
-- given -- the next taken is to that request, by its number -- and how
-- many are written to their end; which answer's sender may send, -1
-- none; what is put there to end that answer where the server stops;
-- whether nothing was sent since a stream's last heartbeat; whether the
-- server has stopped, and whether the connection is closed; and what is
-- done of the answer going on as it closes, its handler's `gone`)
type Outbox (e : HFunctor) (err : Type) =
  { queue : List (Out e err)
  , pending : Int
  , busy : Bool
  , over : Bool
  , given : Int
  , answered : Int
  , sender : Int
  , halt : List (Out e err)
  , quiet : Bool
  , stopping : Bool
  , closed : Bool
  , leave : Co e Unit
  }

-- (a connection's inbox: how many requests are begun -- some of each
-- read -- whether the connection is closed, what a stream's read kept
-- of the next request, and whether a stream goes on, what is read kept
-- for after it)
type Inbox = { asked : Int, closed : Bool, kept : Bytes, streaming : Bool }

-- (a connection, as the server keeps it: its socket, its number among
-- the server's, where its other side is, and its cowns)
type Connection (e : HFunctor) (rg : Type) (err : Type) =
  { socket : With rg Net.TcpStream
  , number : Int
  , peer : Net.Address
  , inbox : BOC.Cown Inbox
  , outbox : BOC.Cown (Outbox e err)
  }

-- (the server's cown: its connections open, by their numbers; the next
-- number; and whether it has stopped)
type Served (e : HFunctor) (rg : Type) (err : Type) =
  { open : OrdMap.OrdMap Int (Connection e rg err)
  , next : Int
  , stopped : Bool
  }

-- (what every behaviour of the server knows: the time limits, a
-- silence's and a head's, the most bytes a connection may have pending,
-- a heartbeat's and a ping's time, the server's cown, and the handler,
-- of the server's world, which may throw an `err`)
type Serving (e : HFunctor) (rg : Type) (err : Type) =
  { idle : Int
  , headerWithin : Int
  , most : Int
  , heartbeat : Int
  , ping : Int
  , served : BOC.Cown (Served e rg err)
  , handler :
        HTTP.ServerRequest
        -> Responder e err
        -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit
  }

-- (no answer's sender)
noSender : Int
noSender = 0 - 1

-- (a connection's outbox, as it is accepted)
emptyOutbox : {e : HFunctor} -> {err : Type} -> Outbox e err
emptyOutbox =
  { queue = Nil
  , pending = 0
  , busy = False
  , over = False
  , given = 0
  , answered = 0
  , sender = noSender
  , halt = Nil
  , quiet = True
  , stopping = False
  , closed = False
  , leave = pure ()
  }

-- (a connection's inbox, as it is accepted)
emptyInbox : Inbox
emptyInbox =
  { asked = 0, closed = False, kept = Bytes.empty, streaming = False }

-- What the outbox is given
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (`x` put in the outbox, after what is there, its bytes counted)
pushed : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {err : Type}
       -> Out e err
       -> Outbox e err
       -> Outbox e err
pushed (Writes b) o =
  { o | queue = Writes b :: o.queue, pending = o.pending + Bytes.length b }
pushed x o = { o | queue = x :: o.queue }

-- (each of `xs` put in the outbox, in order)
pushedAll : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {err : Type}
          -> List (Out e err)
          -> Outbox e err
          -> Outbox e err
pushedAll [] o = o
pushedAll (x :: xs) o = pushedAll xs (pushed x o)

-- (bytes a sender of answer `g` sent, where it may send: past `most` in
-- all, 0 none, the connection to be closed instead)
queued : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {err : Type}
       -> Int
       -> Int
       -> Bytes
       -> Outbox e err
       -> Outbox e err
queued most g b o =
  if o.sender /= g || Bytes.length b == 0 then o
  else if most > 0 && o.pending + Bytes.length b > most
  then { o | over = True }
  else let q = pushed (Writes b) o
       in { q | quiet = False }

-- (the outbox changed by `f`, in a behaviour on it, of whichever world)
outboxed : {f : HFunctor}
         -> {e : HFunctor}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}}
         -> BOC.Cown (Outbox e err)
         -> (Outbox e err -> Outbox e err)
         -> Co f Unit
outboxed outbox change = do
  with o := outbox do
    o := change o

-- (what ends a stream: its last chunk, where it is chunked, then its
-- end)
endOf : {e : HFunctor} -> {err : Type} -> Way -> List (Out e err)
endOf way =
  if way.chunked then [Writes b["0\r\n\r\n"], Finished way.close]
  else [Finished way.close]

-- (what a stream's sender, of answer `g`, is given: a piece, a chunk
-- where it is chunked -- none of no bytes, which would end the chunks --
-- or its end, after which `gone` is not called)
streamStep : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> Int
           -> Int
           -> Way
           -> Maybe Bytes
           -> Outbox e err
           -> Outbox e err
streamStep most g way (Just b) o =
  if Bytes.length b == 0 then o
  else queued most g (if way.chunked then HTTP.chunk b else b) o
streamStep most g way Nothing o =
  if o.sender == g
  then pushedAll
      (endOf way)
      { o | sender = noSender, halt = Nil, leave = pure () }
  else o

-- (a message as it is written: a frame)
frameBytes : WebSocket.Message -> Bytes
frameBytes m = WebSocket.encodeFrame (WebSocket.frameOf m)

-- (whether a message is a close)
isClose : WebSocket.Message -> Bool
isClose (CloseMessage code why) = True
isClose _ = False

-- (what a socket's sender, of answer `g`, is given: a message, a close
-- the last, after which the connection is closed where the server
-- stops; or its end, a close, `1000`)
socketStep : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> Int
           -> Int
           -> Maybe WebSocket.Message
           -> Outbox e err
           -> Outbox e err
socketStep most g (Just m) o =
  let q = queued most g (frameBytes m) o
  in if isClose m && o.sender == g then { q | sender = noSender, halt = [Shut] }
  else q
socketStep most g Nothing o = socketStep most g (Just (CloseMessage 1000 "")) o

-- (a sender on the connection's outbox: each thing given it, `step`, in
-- a behaviour there, of whichever world it is sent from)
senderOn : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {a : Type}
         -> BOC.Cown (Outbox e err)
         -> Int
         -> (Maybe a -> Outbox e err -> Outbox e err)
         -> Sender a
senderOn outbox number step = MkSender number (\x -> outboxed outbox (step x))

-- (a socket's close, its code, answered, on answer `g`: a close of the
-- same code, unless one was sent, then the connection closed once it is
-- written)
closedBack : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> Int
           -> Int
           -> Outbox e err
           -> Outbox e err
closedBack g code o =
  if o.closed then o
  else if o.sender == g
  then pushed
      Shut
      (pushed
         (Writes (frameBytes (CloseMessage code "")))
         { o | sender = noSender, halt = Nil })
  else pushed Shut { o | halt = Nil }

-- (a socket whose client broke the protocol, on answer `g`: a close of
-- the code, and why, unless one was sent, then the connection closed
-- once it is written)
broke : {e : HFunctor}
      -> {err : Type}
      -> Int
      -> Int
      -> String
      -> Outbox e err
      -> Outbox e err
broke g code why o =
  if o.closed then o
  else if o.sender == g
  then pushed
      Shut
      (pushed
         (Writes (frameBytes (CloseMessage code why)))
         { o | sender = noSender, halt = Nil })
  else pushed Shut { o | halt = Nil }

-- (socket `g`'s client silent for a ping's time: a ping sent; or, a
-- close sent already, the connection closed)
silent : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {err : Type}
       -> Int
       -> Int
       -> Outbox e err
       -> Outbox e err
silent most g o =
  if o.closed then o
  else if o.sender == g
  then queued most g (frameBytes (PingMessage Bytes.empty)) o
  else pushed Shut { o | halt = Nil }

-- (a heartbeat's time passed on stream `g`: its heartbeat, `beat`, put
-- in the outbox, where nothing was since the last)
beaten : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {err : Type}
       -> Int
       -> Int
       -> Bytes
       -> Outbox e err
       -> Outbox e err
beaten most g beat o =
  if o.quiet
  then let q = queued most g beat o
       in { q | quiet = True }
  else { o | quiet = True }

-- (the server stopped, for a connection with a request in hand: its
-- answer going on ended, the connection closed after what ends it)
halting : {e : HFunctor} -> {err : Type} -> Outbox e err -> Outbox e err
halting o =
  let h = pushedAll o.halt o
  in { h
     | stopping = True
     , halt = Nil
     , sender = if List.null o.halt then o.sender else noSender
     }

-- (whether a header says its body is server-sent events)
isEventStream : Pair String String -> Bool
isEventStream (MkPair n v) =
  HTTP.sameName n "Content-Type"
  && HTTP.sameName (strSlice (trim v) 0 17) "text/event-stream"

-- (a stream's heartbeat, of these headers: an empty comment, where they
-- are events' and there is a heartbeat's time; none else)
beatOf : Int -> List (Pair String String) -> Bytes
beatOf heartbeat headers =
  if heartbeat > 0 && List.any isEventStream headers
  then SSE.event [Comment ""]
  else Bytes.empty

-- The answer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (what comes after a response written: the connection closed, or its
-- next request read, from what was read after this one)
afterWhole : {e : HFunctor} -> {err : Type} -> Bool -> Bytes -> Out e err
afterWhole True rest = Shut
afterWhole False rest = ReadOn rest

-- (a response, whole, to `r`, put in the outbox: the connection closed
-- after where the request or the response asks, or the server stops)
whole : {e : HFunctor}
      -> {err : Type}
      -> HTTP.ServerRequest
      -> Bytes
      -> HTTP.Response
      -> Outbox e err
      -> Outbox e err
whole r rest res o =
  let close =
    o.stopping
    || not (HTTP.keptAlive r)
    || HTTP.connectionSays "close" res.headers
  in pushed
    (afterWhole close rest)
    (pushed
       (Writes
          (HTTP.responseBytes
             (HTTP.connectionWord r close)
             (not (strEq r.method "HEAD"))
             res))
       o)

-- (a stream, answer `n` to `r`, put in the outbox, `chunked` whether
-- in chunks, `close` whether its connection is closed after it: its
-- head, then the stream begun; to a `HEAD`, or of a status with no
-- body, its head alone, the handler not given it)
streamedAs : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> Int
           -> Int
           -> HTTP.ServerRequest
           -> Bytes
           -> HTTP.Head
           -> Streaming e err
           -> Bool
           -> Bool
           -> Outbox e err
           -> Outbox e err
streamedAs heartbeat n r rest h s chunked close o =
  let wrote =
    pushed
      (Writes
         (HTTP.streamedHead
            (HTTP.connectionWord r close)
            (chunked && not (HTTP.bodiless h.status))
            h))
      o
  in if HTTP.bodiless h.status || strEq r.method "HEAD"
  then pushed (afterWhole close rest) wrote
  else pushed
      (Opens
         n
         rest
         { chunked = chunked
         , close = close
         , beat = beatOf heartbeat h.headers
         }
         s)
      wrote

-- (... in chunks to HTTP/1.1, the connection closed after it to
-- HTTP/1.0, or where the request or the head asks, or the server
-- stops)
streamed : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> Int
         -> Int
         -> HTTP.ServerRequest
         -> Bytes
         -> HTTP.Head
         -> Streaming e err
         -> Outbox e err
         -> Outbox e err
streamed heartbeat n r rest h s o =
  let chunked = strEq r.version "HTTP/1.1"
  in streamedAs
    heartbeat
    n
    r
    rest
    h
    s
    chunked
    (o.stopping
     || not chunked
     || not (HTTP.keptAlive r)
     || HTTP.connectionSays "close" h.headers)
    o

-- (a socket's handshake checked: refused, its refusal written; else its
-- `101` written, then the socket begun)
upgraded : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> Int
         -> HTTP.ServerRequest
         -> Bytes
         -> Socket e err
         -> WebSocket.Handshake
         -> Outbox e err
         -> Outbox e err
upgraded n r rest s (Refuse res) o = whole r rest res o
upgraded n r rest s (Accept key) o =
  pushed
    (Upgrades n rest s)
    (pushed
       (Writes
          (HTTP.takeoverHead
             { status = 101, headers = WebSocket.upgradeHeaders key }))
       o)

answerOf : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> Int
         -> Int
         -> HTTP.ServerRequest
         -> Bytes
         -> Answer e err
         -> Outbox e err
         -> Outbox e err
answerOf heartbeat n r rest (Whole res) o = whole r rest res o
answerOf heartbeat n r rest (Streamed h s) o = streamed heartbeat n r rest h s o
answerOf heartbeat n r rest (Upgraded s) o =
  upgraded n r rest s (WebSocket.handshake r) o

-- (the handler's answer to request `n`, `r`, what was read after it
-- `rest`: taken where it is the first, and the connection open)
respondedTo : {e : HFunctor}
            -> {err : Type}
            -> Int
            -> Int
            -> HTTP.ServerRequest
            -> Bytes
            -> Answer e err
            -> Outbox e err
            -> Outbox e err
respondedTo heartbeat n r rest a o =
  if o.closed || o.given /= n then o
  else answerOf heartbeat n r rest a { o | given = n + 1 }

-- Reading
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (how far a request is read: its head not yet -- the last bytes looked
-- through for its end, three at most, kept, for an end read in two
-- pieces -- or its head, its body to come, from where that starts; or
-- its head, its body sent in chunks, read so far)
data Part
  = InHead Bytes
  | InBody HTTP.ServerRequest Int
  | InChunks HTTP.ServerRequest HTTP.Chunks

-- (when the head being read is to be read by, on the monotonic clock,
-- in milliseconds: at no time -- a body is read, or there is no limit
-- -- within so many of the next read's start, or by then)
data HeadBy = Whenever | Within Int | By Int

-- (what is read of a request: the last read first, `got` bytes in all,
-- how many in all are wanted before it is read again, how far that is,
-- when its head is to be read by, and whether a `100 Continue` was put
-- in the outbox for it)
type Progress =
  { parts : List Bytes
  , got : Int
  , want : Int
  , part : Part
  , by : HeadBy
  , continued : Bool
  }

-- (when a head begun is to be read by, given `headerWithin`: 0, at no
-- time)
headStart : Int -> HeadBy
headStart 0 = Whenever
headStart within = Within within

-- (a request of which nothing is read yet)
begun : HeadBy -> Progress
begun by =
  { parts = Nil
  , got = 0
  , want = 1
  , part = InHead Bytes.empty
  , by = by
  , continued = False
  }

-- (whether nothing of the request is read yet)
unread : Part -> Int -> Bool
unread (InHead tail) got = got == 0
unread _ got = False

-- (how much the next read asks for: what is wanted still, a body's, or
-- `readSize`, whichever is more)
sizeFor : Progress -> Int
sizeFor pg =
  if pg.want - pg.got > HTTP.readSize then pg.want - pg.got
  else HTTP.readSize

-- (whether a read or a write failed for its time limit)
isTimeout : Net.NetError -> Bool
isTimeout why = strEq why.name "ETIMEDOUT"

-- (bytes kept, the last first, as one)
leftOf : List Bytes -> Bytes
leftOf [b] = b
leftOf parts = Bytes.concat (List.reverse parts)

-- (whether the client waits for `100 Continue` before it sends the body
-- of the request whose head is read)
expectsOf : Maybe HTTP.ServerRequest -> Bool
expectsOf Nothing = False
expectsOf (Just r) = HTTP.expects r

-- (the request whose head is read, where it is)
wantedOf : HTTP.Wanted -> Maybe HTTP.ServerRequest
wantedOf (MoreHead want) = Nothing
wantedOf (MoreBody r want) = Just r
wantedOf (MoreChunks r ch) = Just r

-- (what is read of a request, the head read, `buf` all that is read of
-- it, the head's blank line at `i`, as what the request still wants
-- says: so many bytes in all, its body from `i + 4` on; or its body's
-- chunks, which hold what is read of them)
wanting : Progress -> Bytes -> Int -> HTTP.Wanted -> Progress
wanting pg buf i (MoreHead want) =
  { pg
  | parts = [buf]
  , got = Bytes.length buf
  , want = want
  , part = InHead Bytes.empty
  , by = Whenever
  }
wanting pg buf i (MoreBody r want) =
  { pg
  | parts = [buf]
  , got = Bytes.length buf
  , want = want
  , part = InBody r (i + 4)
  , by = Whenever
  }
wanting pg buf i (MoreChunks r ch) =
  { pg | parts = Nil, got = 0, want = 0, part = InChunks r ch, by = Whenever }

-- The acts
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (the next connection accepted, and where its other side is: `Left`
-- where none is accepted; `Nothing` where it was, but closed at once,
-- its other side gone)
acceptedFrom : {e : HFunctor}
             -> {rg : Type}
             -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
             -> With rg Net.TcpListener
             -> Co
               e
               (Either
                  Net.NetError
                  (Maybe (With rg Net.TcpStream, Net.Address)))
acceptedFrom l = do
  r <- Net.accepted l
  case r of
    Left err -> pure (Left err)
    Right c -> do
      p <- Net.peer c
      case p of
        Right a -> pure (Right (Just (c, a)))
        Left err -> do
          _ <- Net.close c
          pure (Right Nothing)

-- (what a read came to: its bytes -- none at the connection's end --
-- and when the head is to be read by after it; or nothing, the read
-- failed, or the head's time passed)
data Arrived = Came HeadBy Bytes | Failed

came : HeadBy -> Either Net.NetError Bytes -> Arrived
came by (Right b) = Came by b
came by (Left err) = Failed

-- (the monotonic clock, in milliseconds)
millisecondsNow : {e : HFunctor} -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}} -> Co e Int
millisecondsNow =
  bind Time.monotonic (\d -> pure (Duration.totalNanoseconds d / 1000000))

-- (the shorter of two time limits, 0 none)
shorter : Int -> Int -> Int
shorter 0 left = left
shorter idle left = if idle < left then idle else left

-- (a read, `now`, of `size` bytes at most, within `idle` milliseconds,
-- and by `until`: where that has passed, none)
readUntil : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {rg : Type}
          -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
          -> Int
          -> Int
          -> Int
          -> With rg Net.TcpStream
          -> Int
          -> Co e Arrived
readUntil idle until now c size =
  if now >= until then pure Failed
  else bind
      (Net.read (timeout := shorter idle (until - now)) c size)
      (\r -> pure (came (By until) r))

-- (a read of `size` bytes at most, within `idle` milliseconds, and where
-- a head is read by a time, by then: its time taken at this read's start
-- where it is the head's first)
readBy : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {rg : Type}
       -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
       -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
       -> Int
       -> HeadBy
       -> With rg Net.TcpStream
       -> Int
       -> Co e Arrived
readBy idle Whenever c size =
  bind (Net.read (timeout := idle) c size) (\r -> pure (came Whenever r))
readBy idle (Within within) c size =
  bind millisecondsNow (\now -> readUntil idle (now + within) now c size)
readBy idle (By until) c size =
  bind millisecondsNow (\now -> readUntil idle until now c size)

-- (what the client still sends read and let go of, `got` bytes of it
-- already, until it ends or there has been `drainMost` of it)
drained : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {rg : Type}
        -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
        -> Int
        -> With rg Net.TcpStream
        -> Int
        -> Co e Unit
drained idle c got =
  if got >= HTTP.drainMost then pure ()
  else do
    r <- Net.read (timeout := idle) c HTTP.readSize
    case r of
      Right b ->
        if Bytes.length b == 0 then pure ()
        else drained idle c (got + Bytes.length b)
      Left err -> pure ()

-- (a refusal written, then this side shut down, so that the client reads
-- it to its end, and what the client still sends drained, so that the
-- connection closed with bytes unread -- a reset, which may come before
-- the client has read the refusal -- is not what it is sent)
refused : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {rg : Type}
        -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
        -> Int
        -> With rg Net.TcpStream
        -> Int
        -> Co e Unit
refused idle c status = do
  r <-
    Net.write
      (timeout := idle)
      c
      (HTTP.responseBytes
         "close"
         True
         (HTTP.plain status (HTTP.reason status ^ "\n")))
  case r of
    Right u -> do
      _ <- Net.shutdown c
      drained idle c 0
    Left err -> pure ()

-- Closing
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (the connection's socket closed, and the connection taken from the
-- server's)
closing : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {rg : Type}
        -> {err : Type}
        -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
        -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
        -> Serving e rg err
        -> Connection e rg err
        -> Co e Unit
closing sg cn = do
  with sv := sg.served
       _ := BOC.await (Net.close cn.socket) do
    sv := { sv | open = OrdMap.delete sv.open cn.number }

-- (the connection closed, from a behaviour holding its outbox: its
-- socket closed, the handler's `gone` called where an answer goes on,
-- and the outbox closed, nothing written after)
shut : {e : HFunctor}
     -> {rg : Type}
     -> {err : Type}
     -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
     -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
     -> Serving e rg err
     -> Connection e rg err
     -> Outbox e err
     -> Co e (Outbox e err)
shut sg cn o = do
  closing sg cn
  o.leave
  pure
      { o
      | queue = Nil
      , pending = 0
      , sender = noSender
      , halt = Nil
      , closed = True
      , leave = pure ()
      }

-- (the connection closed, by a behaviour on both its cowns, where it is
-- not yet: what comes back to either after does nothing)
closer : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {rg : Type}
       -> {err : Type}
       -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
       -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
       -> Serving e rg err
       -> Connection e rg err
       -> Co e Unit
closer sg cn = do
  with i := cn.inbox
       o := cn.outbox do
    if o.closed then pure ()
    else do
      i := { i | closed = True }
      o := (<- shut sg cn o)

-- (a refusal written, what the client still sends drained, then the
-- connection closed)
refusing : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {rg : Type}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
         -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
         -> Serving e rg err
         -> Connection e rg err
         -> Int
         -> Co e Unit
refusing sg cn status = do
  with _ := BOC.await (refused sg.idle cn.socket status) do
    closer sg cn

-- (a `100 Continue` put in the outbox)
continuing : {f : HFunctor}
           -> {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: f}}
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Co f Unit
continuing cn =
  outboxed
    cn.outbox
    (\o ->
       if o.closed then o
       else pushed (Writes b["HTTP/1.1 100 Continue\r\n\r\n"]) o)

-- The inbox's behaviours: a request read
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

mutual
  -- (more of the request read, `size` bytes at most, in `idle`
  -- milliseconds, and where its head is read, by `pg.by`: what it comes
  -- to taken, in a behaviour on the inbox, with what was read)
  reading : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {rg : Type}
          -> {err : Type}
          -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
          -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
          -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
          -> Serving e rg err
          -> Connection e rg err
          -> Int
          -> Progress
          -> Co e Unit
  reading sg cn size pg = do
    with i := cn.inbox
         got := BOC.await (readBy sg.idle pg.by cn.socket size) do
      i := (<- readCame sg cn i pg got)

  -- (what a read came to, where the connection is open: the request's
  -- first bytes counted)
  readCame : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Inbox
           -> Progress
           -> Arrived
           -> Co e Inbox
  readCame sg cn i pg got =
    if i.closed then pure i
    else if unread pg.part pg.got
    then cameTo sg cn { i | asked = i.asked + 1 } pg got
    else cameTo sg cn i pg got

  -- (what a read came to: its bytes taken; at its end, or failed, the
  -- connection closed)
  cameTo : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {rg : Type}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
         -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
         -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
         -> Serving e rg err
         -> Connection e rg err
         -> Inbox
         -> Progress
         -> Arrived
         -> Co e Inbox
  cameTo sg cn i pg (Came after b) =
    if Bytes.length b == 0
    then do
      closer sg cn
      pure i
    else took sg cn i { pg | by = after } pg.part b
  cameTo sg cn i pg Failed = do
    closer sg cn
    pure i

  -- (bytes `b` read, taken as far as they go, where the request is,
  -- `part`)
  took : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {rg : Type}
       -> {err : Type}
       -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
       -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
       -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
       -> Serving e rg err
       -> Connection e rg err
       -> Inbox
       -> Progress
       -> Part
       -> Bytes
       -> Co e Inbox
  took sg cn i pg (InHead tail) b = looked sg cn i pg tail b
  took sg cn i pg (InBody r at) b =
    advanced
      sg
      cn
      i
      { pg | parts = b :: pg.parts, got = pg.got + Bytes.length b }
  took sg cn i pg (InChunks r ch) b =
    dechunked sg cn i pg r (HTTP.feedChunks ch b)

  -- (what a piece of a body sent in chunks came to: the request, all
  -- read, handed to the handler; more read; or the request refused)
  dechunked : {e : HFunctor}
            -> {rg : Type}
            -> {err : Type}
            -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
            -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
            -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
            -> Serving e rg err
            -> Connection e rg err
            -> Inbox
            -> Progress
            -> HTTP.ServerRequest
            -> HTTP.Dechunked
            -> Co e Inbox
  dechunked sg cn i pg r (BodyRead body rest) =
    answered sg cn i (Asked { r | body = body } rest)
  dechunked sg cn i pg r (ChunksToCome ch) = do
    reading sg cn HTTP.readSize { pg | part = InChunks r ch }
    pure i
  dechunked sg cn i pg r (ChunksRefused status) =
    answered sg cn i (Refused status)

  -- (bytes `b` read of a head, its end looked for in them alone, and in
  -- `tail`, the last bytes read before them: where it is found, the
  -- head parsed, once; else more read, or, the head too long, refused)
  looked : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {rg : Type}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
         -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
         -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
         -> Serving e rg err
         -> Connection e rg err
         -> Inbox
         -> Progress
         -> Bytes
         -> Bytes
         -> Co e Inbox
  looked sg cn i pg tail b = do
    let at = HTTP.blankIn tail b 0
    let read = { pg | parts = b :: pg.parts, got = pg.got + Bytes.length b }
    if at >= 0 then headed sg cn i read (pg.got - Bytes.length tail + at)
    else if read.got > HTTP.headMost
    then do
      refusing sg cn 431
      pure i
    else do
      reading
          sg
          cn
          HTTP.headPiece
          { read | want = read.got + 1, part = InHead (HTTP.lastThree tail b) }
      pure i

  -- (the head read, its blank line at `at` of what is read: parsed, and
  -- its body read, a `100 Continue` put in the outbox first where the
  -- client waits for one; or the request handed to the handler, or
  -- refused)
  headed : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {rg : Type}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
         -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
         -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
         -> Serving e rg err
         -> Connection e rg err
         -> Inbox
         -> Progress
         -> Int
         -> Co e Inbox
  headed sg cn i pg at = do
    let buf = leftOf pg.parts
    case HTTP.requestIn (from := at) cn.peer buf of
      Left wanted -> do
        let more = wanting pg buf at wanted
        if expectsOf (wantedOf wanted) && not pg.continued
        then do
          continuing cn
          reading sg cn (sizeFor more) { more | continued = True }
        else reading sg cn (sizeFor more) more
        pure i
      Right g -> answered sg cn i g

  -- (what is read of a request's body: more read, where too little is;
  -- else the request handed to the handler)
  advanced : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Inbox
           -> Progress
           -> Co e Inbox
  advanced sg cn i pg =
    if pg.got < pg.want
    then do
      reading sg cn (sizeFor pg) pg
      pure i
    else bodied sg cn i pg pg.part

  -- (the request whose head is read, `part`, its body all read: handed
  -- to the handler, what was read after it kept)
  bodied : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {rg : Type}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
         -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
         -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
         -> Serving e rg err
         -> Connection e rg err
         -> Inbox
         -> Progress
         -> Part
         -> Co e Inbox
  bodied sg cn i pg (InBody r at) = do
    let buf = leftOf pg.parts
    answered
        sg
        cn
        i
        (Asked
           { r | body = Bytes.slice buf at pg.want }
           (Bytes.slice buf pg.want (Bytes.length buf)))
  bodied sg cn i pg (InHead tail) =
    looked sg cn i { pg | parts = Nil, got = 0 } Bytes.empty (leftOf pg.parts)
  bodied sg cn i pg (InChunks r ch) = dechunked sg cn i pg r (ChunksToCome ch)

  -- (what a request read came to: handed to the handler, with a
  -- responder that puts its answer in the outbox -- what was read after
  -- it, where there is some, the next request begun -- or refused, or
  -- the connection's end)
  answered : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Inbox
           -> HTTP.Got
           -> Co e Inbox
  answered sg cn i (Asked r rest) = do
    let n = i.asked - 1
    outcome <-
      attempt
        err
        (sg.handler
           r
           (\a -> outboxed cn.outbox (respondedTo sg.heartbeat n r rest a)))
    case outcome of
      Right u ->
        pure (if Bytes.length rest > 0 then { i | asked = i.asked + 1 } else i)
      Left thrown -> do
        closer sg cn
        pure i
  answered sg cn i (Refused status) = do
    refusing sg cn status
    pure i
  answered sg cn i Ended = do
    closer sg cn
    pure i

-- (the next request read, a response written: from what was read after
-- the last, where there is some, else from a read)
nextOn : {e : HFunctor}
       -> {rg : Type}
       -> {err : Type}
       -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
       -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
       -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
       -> Serving e rg err
       -> Connection e rg err
       -> Bytes
       -> Co e Unit
nextOn sg cn rest =
  if Bytes.length rest == 0
  then reading sg cn HTTP.readSize (begun (headStart sg.headerWithin))
  else do
    with i := cn.inbox do
      if i.closed then pure ()
      else do
        i :=
          (<- looked
            sg
            cn
            i
            (begun (headStart sg.headerWithin))
            Bytes.empty
            rest)

-- The inbox's behaviours: a stream's reads
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

mutual
  -- (stream `g`'s connection read, a piece at a time, within `ms`
  -- milliseconds: what it comes to taken in a behaviour on the inbox)
  listening : {e : HFunctor}
            -> {rg : Type}
            -> {err : Type}
            -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
            -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
            -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
            -> Serving e rg err
            -> Connection e rg err
            -> Int
            -> Int
            -> Bytes
            -> Co e Unit
  listening sg cn g ms beat = do
    with i := cn.inbox
         got := BOC.await (Net.read (timeout := ms) cn.socket HTTP.headPiece) do
      i := (<- listened sg cn g ms beat i got)

  -- (what a read of stream `g`'s connection came to: while the stream
  -- goes on, the stream's; once it is finished, the next request's)
  listened : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Int
           -> Int
           -> Bytes
           -> Inbox
           -> Either Net.NetError Bytes
           -> Co e Inbox
  listened sg cn g ms beat i got =
    if i.closed then pure i
    else if i.streaming then streamHeard sg cn g ms beat i got
    else afterStream sg cn i got

  -- (what a read of stream `g`'s connection came to while it goes on:
  -- the client's end, the connection closed; bytes, the next request's,
  -- kept, the connection read no further; nothing, the time passed, a
  -- heartbeat put in the outbox where there is one, and the connection
  -- read on)
  streamHeard : {e : HFunctor}
              -> {rg : Type}
              -> {err : Type}
              -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
              -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
              -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
              -> Serving e rg err
              -> Connection e rg err
              -> Int
              -> Int
              -> Bytes
              -> Inbox
              -> Either Net.NetError Bytes
              -> Co e Inbox
  streamHeard sg cn g ms beat i (Right b) =
    if Bytes.length b == 0
    then do
      closer sg cn
      pure i
    else pure { i | kept = b, asked = i.asked + 1 }
  streamHeard sg cn g ms beat i (Left why) =
    if isTimeout why
    then do
      if Bytes.length beat > 0 then outboxed cn.outbox (beaten sg.most g beat)
      else pure ()
      listening sg cn g ms beat
      pure i
    else do
      closer sg cn
      pure i

  -- (what a read a stream waited on came to, the stream finished: the
  -- next request's start, or, nothing come in its time, the next
  -- request read; the client's end, or a failure, the connection
  -- closed)
  afterStream : {e : HFunctor}
              -> {rg : Type}
              -> {err : Type}
              -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
              -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
              -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
              -> Serving e rg err
              -> Connection e rg err
              -> Inbox
              -> Either Net.NetError Bytes
              -> Co e Inbox
  afterStream sg cn i (Right b) =
    readCame
      sg
      cn
      i
      (begun (headStart sg.headerWithin))
      (Came (headStart sg.headerWithin) b)
  afterStream sg cn i (Left why) =
    if isTimeout why
    then do
      nextOn sg cn Bytes.empty
      pure i
    else do
      closer sg cn
      pure i

-- (how long a stream's connection is read for at a time: a heartbeat's
-- time, where it has one, else `idle`)
listenFor : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {rg : Type}
          -> {err : Type}
          -> Serving e rg err
          -> Bytes
          -> Int
listenFor sg beat = if Bytes.length beat > 0 then sg.heartbeat else sg.idle

-- (stream `g` begun, on the inbox: what was read after its request
-- kept for after it, else its connection read)
streamHeld : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Int
           -> Bytes
           -> Bytes
           -> Co e Unit
streamHeld sg cn g beat rest = do
  with i := cn.inbox do
    if i.closed then pure ()
    else if Bytes.length rest > 0
    then do
      i := { i | streaming = True, kept = rest }
    else do
      listening sg cn g (listenFor sg beat) beat
      i := { i | streaming = True }

-- (a stream finished and written, on the inbox: the next request read
-- from what was kept, where there is some; else the read waiting is
-- its)
streamDone : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Co e Unit
streamDone sg cn = do
  with i := cn.inbox do
    if i.closed then pure ()
    else if Bytes.length i.kept > 0
    then do
      i :=
        (<- looked
          sg
          cn
          { i | streaming = False, kept = Bytes.empty }
          (begun (headStart sg.headerWithin))
          Bytes.empty
          i.kept)
    else do
      i := { i | streaming = False }

-- The inbox's behaviours: a socket's reads
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (the sender of the connection's socket, answer `g`)
socketSender : {e : HFunctor}
             -> {rg : Type}
             -> {err : Type}
             -> Serving e rg err
             -> Connection e rg err
             -> Int
             -> Sender WebSocket.Message
socketSender sg cn g = senderOn cn.outbox cn.number (socketStep sg.most g)

-- (a message the client sent on socket `g`: a ping answered, a close
-- answered, the others the handler's; whether to go on, not where the
-- handler threw, its connection closed)
heardOne : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {rg : Type}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
         -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
         -> Serving e rg err
         -> Connection e rg err
         -> Int
         -> Socket e err
         -> WebSocket.Message
         -> Co e Bool
heardOne sg cn g s (PingMessage p) = do
  outboxed cn.outbox (queued sg.most g (frameBytes (PongMessage p)))
  pure True
heardOne sg cn g s (PongMessage p) = pure True
heardOne sg cn g s (CloseMessage code why) = do
  outboxed cn.outbox (closedBack g code)
  pure True
heardOne sg cn g s m = do
  outcome <- attempt err (s.received (socketSender sg cn g) m)
  case outcome of
    Right u -> pure True
    Left thrown -> do
      closer sg cn
      pure False

-- (the messages the client sent on socket `g`, in order, until the
-- handler throws: whether it did not)
heard : {e : HFunctor}
      -> {rg : Type}
      -> {err : Type}
      -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
      -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
      -> Serving e rg err
      -> Connection e rg err
      -> Int
      -> Socket e err
      -> List WebSocket.Message
      -> Co e Bool
heard sg cn g s [] = pure True
heard sg cn g s (m :: ms) = do
  go <- heardOne sg cn g s m
  if go then heard sg cn g s ms else pure False

mutual
  -- (socket `g` read on, `fr` its frames so far, `pinged` whether a ping
  -- is unanswered: what comes in `ping` milliseconds, or that nothing
  -- did, taken in a behaviour on the inbox)
  socketReading : {e : HFunctor}
                -> {rg : Type}
                -> {err : Type}
                -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
                -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
                -> Serving e rg err
                -> Connection e rg err
                -> Int
                -> Socket e err
                -> WebSocket.Reader
                -> Bool
                -> Co e Unit
  socketReading sg cn g s fr pinged = do
    with i := cn.inbox
         got :=
             BOC.await
               (Net.read (timeout := sg.ping) cn.socket HTTP.readSize) do
      if i.closed then pure () else socketCame sg cn g s fr pinged got

  -- (what a read of socket `g` came to: its frames' messages heard,
  -- and read on unless a close has come or the client broke the
  -- protocol; where nothing came for `ping` milliseconds, a ping sent,
  -- or, one sent already, the connection closed; where the client has
  -- gone, the connection closed)
  socketCame : {e : HFunctor}
             -> {rg : Type}
             -> {err : Type}
             -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
             -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
             -> Serving e rg err
             -> Connection e rg err
             -> Int
             -> Socket e err
             -> WebSocket.Reader
             -> Bool
             -> Either Net.NetError Bytes
             -> Co e Unit
  socketCame sg cn g s fr pinged (Right b) =
    if Bytes.length b == 0 then closer sg cn
    else fedTo sg cn g s (WebSocket.feed fr b)
  socketCame sg cn g s fr pinged (Left why) =
    if isTimeout why && not pinged
    then do
      outboxed cn.outbox (silent sg.most g)
      socketReading sg cn g s fr True
    else closer sg cn

  -- (what the frames read came to: their messages heard, and the
  -- socket read on; or, after the client broke the protocol, closed)
  fedTo : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {rg : Type}
        -> {err : Type}
        -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
        -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
        -> Serving e rg err
        -> Connection e rg err
        -> Int
        -> Socket e err
        -> WebSocket.Fed
        -> Co e Unit
  fedTo sg cn g s (Going ms r) = do
    go <- heard sg cn g s ms
    if go && not r.closed then socketReading sg cn g s r False else pure ()
  fedTo sg cn g s (Failing ms code why) = do
    go <- heard sg cn g s ms
    if go then outboxed cn.outbox (broke g code why) else pure ()

-- (socket `g` begun, on the inbox: what was read after its request
-- taken as its first frames, else its connection read)
socketHeld : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Int
           -> Socket e err
           -> Bytes
           -> Co e Unit
socketHeld sg cn g s rest = do
  with i := cn.inbox do
    if i.closed then pure ()
    else if Bytes.length rest > 0
    then socketCame sg cn g s WebSocket.reader False (Right rest)
    else socketReading sg cn g s WebSocket.reader False

-- The outbox's writer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (whether the writer has something to do: the connection closed, more
-- than the most sent, or, no write awaited, something put in the
-- outbox)
ready : {e : HFunctor} -> {err : Type} -> Outbox e err -> Bool
ready o = o.closed || o.over || (not o.busy && not (List.null o.queue))

-- (what writing `n` bytes came to: no write awaited any more, so the
-- writer, waiting, does what was put in the outbox meanwhile; or, not
-- written, the connection closed)
afterWrite : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Int
           -> Outbox e err
           -> Either Net.NetError Unit
           -> Co e (Outbox e err)
afterWrite sg cn n o (Right u) =
  if o.closed then pure o
  else pure { o | busy = False, pending = o.pending - n }
afterWrite sg cn n o (Left why) =
  if o.closed then pure o
  else do
    closer sg cn
    pure o

-- (bytes written by a write awaited, in a behaviour on the outbox once
-- it is done)
wroteRest : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {rg : Type}
          -> {err : Type}
          -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
          -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
          -> Serving e rg err
          -> Connection e rg err
          -> Bytes
          -> Co e Unit
wroteRest sg cn bytes = do
  with o := cn.outbox
       sent := BOC.await (Net.write (timeout := sg.idle) cn.socket bytes) do
    o := (<- afterWrite sg cn (Bytes.length bytes) o sent)

-- (the bytes at the front of `items`, the last first, and what is
-- after them)
leading : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {err : Type}
        -> List (Out e err)
        -> List Bytes
        -> Pair (List Bytes) (List (Out e err))
leading (Writes b :: rest) acc = leading rest (b :: acc)
leading items acc = MkPair acc items

-- (the sender of the connection's stream, answer `g`)
streamSender : {e : HFunctor}
             -> {rg : Type}
             -> {err : Type}
             -> Serving e rg err
             -> Connection e rg err
             -> Int
             -> Way
             -> Sender Bytes
streamSender sg cn g way =
  senderOn cn.outbox cn.number (streamStep sg.most g way)

-- (a handler's `gone`, what it throws let go of)
goneWith : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {a : Type}
         -> (Sender a -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit)
         -> Sender a
         -> Co e Unit
goneWith gone out = bind (attempt err (gone out)) (\o -> pure ())

mutual
  -- (the writer, waiting on the outbox, `once`, until it has something
  -- to do: then it does it, in a behaviour of the server's world)
  writing : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {rg : Type}
          -> {err : Type}
          -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
          -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
          -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
          -> Serving e rg err
          -> Connection e rg err
          -> Co e Unit
  writing sg cn = do
    with o := cn.outbox once ready o do
      o := (<- wrote sg cn o)

  -- (the writer's turn come: the connection closed, nothing more done;
  -- else what is in the outbox done, in order)
  wrote : {e : HFunctor}
        -> {rg : Type}
        -> {err : Type}
        -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
        -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
        -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
        -> Serving e rg err
        -> Connection e rg err
        -> Outbox e err
        -> Co e (Outbox e err)
  wrote sg cn o =
    if o.closed then pure o
    else pumped sg cn { o | queue = Nil } (List.reverse o.queue)

  -- (`items` done, in order, taken from the outbox: past the most, the
  -- connection closed instead)
  pumped : {e : HFunctor}
         -> {rg : Type}
         -> {err : Type}
         -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
         -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
         -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
         -> Serving e rg err
         -> Connection e rg err
         -> Outbox e err
         -> List (Out e err)
         -> Co e (Outbox e err)
  pumped sg cn o items =
    if o.over
    then do
      closer sg cn
      pure o
    else pumpedOn sg cn o items

  -- (... all done, what was put in the outbox meanwhile done, else the
  -- writer waiting again; bytes at the front written, as one)
  pumpedOn : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Outbox e err
           -> List (Out e err)
           -> Co e (Outbox e err)
  pumpedOn sg cn o [] =
    if List.null o.queue
    then do
      writing sg cn
      pure o
    else pumped sg cn { o | queue = Nil } (List.reverse o.queue)
  pumpedOn sg cn o items = written sg cn o (leading items Nil)

  -- (the bytes at the front, where there are any, written: what the
  -- socket takes at once; else the first thing done)
  written : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {rg : Type}
          -> {err : Type}
          -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
          -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
          -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
          -> Serving e rg err
          -> Connection e rg err
          -> Outbox e err
          -> Pair (List Bytes) (List (Out e err))
          -> Co e (Outbox e err)
  written sg cn o (MkPair [] (x :: rest)) = act sg cn o x rest
  written sg cn o (MkPair [] []) = pumpedOn sg cn o []
  written sg cn o (MkPair bs rest) = do
    let bytes = leftOf bs
    now <- Net.writeNow cn.socket bytes
    writtenNow sg cn o rest bytes now

  -- (what writing `bytes` at once came to: all of them written, the
  -- rest of `items` done; some, the rest of them written by a write
  -- awaited, what is still to do put back in the outbox before what was
  -- put there since, and the writer waiting again, for the most passed
  -- too, while the write is awaited; or, not written, the connection
  -- closed)
  writtenNow : {e : HFunctor}
             -> {rg : Type}
             -> {err : Type}
             -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
             -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
             -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
             -> Serving e rg err
             -> Connection e rg err
             -> Outbox e err
             -> List (Out e err)
             -> Bytes
             -> Either Net.NetError Int
             -> Co e (Outbox e err)
  writtenNow sg cn o items bytes (Right n) =
    if n == Bytes.length bytes
    then pumped sg cn { o | pending = o.pending - n } items
    else do
      wroteRest sg cn (Bytes.slice bytes n (Bytes.length bytes))
      writing sg cn
      pure
          { o
          | busy = True
          , pending = o.pending - n
          , queue = List.append o.queue (List.reverse items)
          }
  writtenNow sg cn o items bytes (Left why) = do
    closer sg cn
    pure o

  -- (one thing done, what is before it written, then the rest of
  -- `items`: a response's next request read, or, the server stopped, the
  -- connection closed; the connection closed; a stream or a socket
  -- begun; a stream's end, the next request read, or the connection
  -- closed)
  act : {e : HFunctor}
      -> {rg : Type}
      -> {err : Type}
      -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
      -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
      -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
      -> Serving e rg err
      -> Connection e rg err
      -> Outbox e err
      -> Out e err
      -> List (Out e err)
      -> Co e (Outbox e err)
  act sg cn o (Writes b) items = pumped sg cn o (Writes b :: items)
  act sg cn o (ReadOn rest) items =
    if o.stopping
    then do
      closer sg cn
      pure { o | answered = o.answered + 1 }
    else do
      nextOn sg cn rest
      pumped sg cn { o | answered = o.answered + 1 } items
  act sg cn o Shut items = do
    closer sg cn
    pure o
  act sg cn o (Opens g rest way s) items = do
    let out = streamSender sg cn g way
    let going =
      { o
      | sender = g
      , halt = endOf way
      , quiet = True
      , leave = goneWith s.gone out
      }
    outcome <- attempt err (s.opened out)
    case outcome of
      Left thrown -> do
        closer sg cn
        pure going
      Right u -> do
        streamHeld
            sg
            cn
            g
            (if way.chunked then HTTP.chunk way.beat else way.beat)
            rest
        pumped sg cn (if o.stopping then halting going else going) items
  act sg cn o (Upgrades g rest s) items = do
    let out = socketSender sg cn g
    let going =
      { o
      | sender = g
      , halt = [Writes (frameBytes (CloseMessage 1001 "")), Shut]
      , leave = goneWith s.gone out
      }
    outcome <- attempt err (s.opened out)
    case outcome of
      Left thrown -> do
        closer sg cn
        pure going
      Right u -> do
        socketHeld sg cn g s rest
        pumped sg cn (if o.stopping then halting going else going) items
  act sg cn o (Finished close) items =
    if close || o.stopping
    then do
      closer sg cn
      pure { o | answered = o.answered + 1 }
    else do
      streamDone sg cn
      pumped sg cn { o | answered = o.answered + 1 } items

-- Stopping
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (the server stopped, for a connection, by a behaviour on both its
-- cowns: closed at once where every request begun is answered, the next
-- waited for; else its answer going on ended, and closed once the
-- request in hand is answered)
stoppedFor : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> Connection e rg err
           -> Co e Unit
stoppedFor sg cn = do
  with i := cn.inbox
       o := cn.outbox do
    if o.closed then pure ()
    else if i.asked == o.answered
    then do
      i := { i | closed = True }
      o := (<- shut sg cn o)
    else do
      o := halting o

stoppedAll : {e : HFunctor}
           -> {rg : Type}
           -> {err : Type}
           -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
           -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
           -> Serving e rg err
           -> List (Pair Int (Connection e rg err))
           -> Co e Unit
stoppedAll sg [] = pure ()
stoppedAll sg (MkPair n cn :: rest) = do
  stoppedFor sg cn
  stoppedAll sg rest

-- (the listener's next connection accepted, and given its cowns, its
-- writer waiting and its first request read; then the one after it;
-- where none is accepted, the server stopped)
accepting : {e : HFunctor}
          -> {rg : Type}
          -> {err : Type}
          -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
          -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
          -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
          -> Serving e rg err
          -> With rg Net.TcpListener
          -> Co e Unit
accepting sg l = do
  with sv := sg.served
       c := BOC.await (acceptedFrom l) do
    case c of
      Right (Just (sock, peer)) -> do
        inbox <- BOC.newCown emptyInbox
        outbox <- BOC.newCown (emptyOutbox : Outbox e err)
        let cn : Connection e rg err =
          { socket = sock
          , number = sv.next
          , peer = peer
          , inbox = inbox
          , outbox = outbox
          }
        sv :=
          { sv
          | open = OrdMap.insert sv.open sv.next cn
          , next = sv.next + 1
          }
        writing sg cn
        reading sg cn HTTP.readSize (begun (headStart sg.headerWithin))
        accepting sg l
      Right Nothing -> accepting sg l
      Left err -> do
        let open = OrdMap.toList sv.open
        sv := { sv | stopped = True }
        stoppedAll sg open

-- | The listener's connections served, each request given to `handle`
-- | with a responder, every wait an act awaited -- so as many
-- | connections are served at once as come, none holding a thread while
-- | it waits.  Comes back at once, the server running in behaviours of
-- | its own, until a connection cannot be accepted (the listener closed,
-- | `EMFILE`): then the connections waiting for a request are closed,
-- | streams ended and sockets closed, and the others once the request
-- | in hand is answered.  A client that sends nothing for `idle`
-- | milliseconds, or takes nothing written in that time, is closed; so
-- | is one that has not sent all of a request's head within
-- | `headerWithin` milliseconds of the connection's accept, or of the
-- | response before it.  A connection with more than `pending` bytes
-- | sent and not yet written is closed.  A stream of events is sent a
-- | comment after `heartbeat` milliseconds of silence; a socket, a
-- | ping after `ping`.  The handler, of the server's world, may throw
-- | an `err`: that connection is closed at once, unanswered, and what
-- | was thrown let go of.
serve : {e : HFunctor}
      -> {rg : Type}
      -> {idle : Int := 30000}
      -> {headerWithin : Int := 30000}
      -> {pending : Int := 1048576}
      -> {heartbeat : Int := 15000}
      -> {ping : Int := 30000}
      -> (err : Type)
      -> {{bc : BOC.boc <: e}}
      -> {{nt : Net.net <: e}}
      -> {{clock : Time.time <: e}}
      -> With rg Net.TcpListener
      -> (HTTP.ServerRequest
          -> Responder e err
          -> Co (exception err <+> e) Unit)
      -> Co e Unit
serve err l handle = do
  served <-
    BOC.newCown
      ({ open = OrdMap.empty, next = 0, stopped = False } : Served e rg err)
  accepting
      { idle = idle
      , headerWithin = headerWithin
      , most = pending
      , heartbeat = heartbeat
      , ping = ping
      , served = served
      , handler = handle
      }
      l
